{"input_text": "Question: I have to calculate fundamental group of disk  $D^2$  minus two points and then fundamental group of disk  $D^2$  minus  $k$  points. I know that fundamental group of  $D^2$  minus one point is trivial. Can you help me? 

Answer: As Christoph pointed out the fundamental group of a disk minus one point is not trivial. It is actually generated by the homotopy class of the loop going around the hole, i.e. we have  $$\pi_1(D^2\setminus \lbrace x \rbrace,x') \cong \mathbb{Z}.$$  By using von Kampen's theorem you can now get a description of the fundamental group of the disk with several holes. 
